        <h1>Hazards of Lunar Surface Exploration: Determining the Immunogenicity/Allergenicity of Lunar Dust</h1>
        <div class="meta-id">Record ID: Immune_Lunar_Dust</div>
      </div>

      <div class="content">
        <div class="action-banner">
          <div class="action-banner-text">
            <strong>Official Source:</strong> National Aeronautics and Space Administration Life Sciences Data Archive
          </div>
          <a href="https://nlsp.nasa.gov/search/data/details/894b6aa6-386d-5094-87c7-8f600aea18d5?dataType=EXPERIMENTS" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="btn">
            View Official Record on NASA NLSP &rarr;
          </a>
        </div>

        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <div>OBJECTIVES:</div><div><span><span><br></span></span></div><div><span><span>There are multiple Apollo program reports of lunar dust (LD) exposure leading to significant upper respiratory symptoms in select crewmembers. Possible mechanisms include particulate irritation, oxidization and release of noxious gas, or legitimate adaptive immune mediated response. Although sterile non-protein matter would not be expected to be an allergen, one Apollo flight surgeon reported increasing symptoms upon repeated exposure, with associated eosinophilia indicative of allergy. Many International Space Station (ISS) crewmembers display a pattern of persistent immune system dysregulation and latent virus reactivation. Some ISS crewmembers manifest atypical respiratory and/or dermatitis symptoms which could have an allergic pathogenesis.&nbsp;</span></span></div><div><span><span>

This study had the following specific aims:
</span></span></div><div><span><span>1)	Determine if in-vitro exposure to lunar dust results in immediate mitogenic activation of any subset of human peripheral blood immune cells. </span></span></div><div><span><span>
2)	Determine if long-term (14 days) exposure of human primary immune cells to lunar dust can induce a T cell, B cell, to Immunoglobulin E (IgE) mediated sensitization. 
</span></span></div><div><br></div><div>APPROACH:</div><div></div><div><span><span><br></span></span></div><div>

<div>This study investigated if LD exposure elicits an IgE
mediated allergic response either to the LD itself or concomitant antigen
exposure during spaceflight. Allergic reactivity could adversely increase
clinical and operational impacts for long-duration lunar astronauts and affect
countermeasure requirements for surface vehicles. Specific aims for this study
were to answer two questions: (1) Does in vitro LD exposure result in increased
histamine from human peripheral blood basophils? (2) Can LD impact the capacity
of CD4+ T helper and/or CD19+ B-cell mediated IgE production? <o:p></o:p></div><div><br></div>

<div>To address these questions, the PI team requested and was approved to receive actual LD samples from the Apollo 16 mission. These samples were used during the study to complete the proposed set of in vitro cell culture experiments, using human whole blood and human peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMC). Cells were co-cultured with cellular mitogens, common recall antigens (Der p1), fine ground silica quartz (as a possible allergenic component of LD), or LD, to study whether LD exposure could alter the generation of selective immune responses associated with clinical allergic reactions.
Measured outputs included supernatant-derived IgE, leukotriene, histamine, and selected cytokines levels. Cellular activation was monitored by assessing activation markers via flow cytometry. EM/X-ray analysis were used to determine cellular interactions with dust particles. <o:p></o:p></div>
<div>A series of validation experiments were initiated in FY22 once the delivery of LD was received. Based on initial experimental findings, the PI team optimized the culture conditions, LD concentrations, and refined the protocol stimuli. Subjects that were recruited were active, healthy test subjects, enrolled and screened by NASA JSC ‘Test Subject Facility’ (TSF). Living immune cells from the blood of these subjects were obtained. These immune cells were purified and then cultured in the presence of common allergens with or without lunar dust. Various factors were measured to determine if LD caused any type of response from the immune cells such as activation or allergy. The measured factors included histamine, Immunoglobulin E, or activation of basophils. <o:p></o:p></div>

<span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);"><br></span></div><div><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">RESULTS:</span>
</div><div></div><div><div><br></div><div>This study aimed to investigate the potential of allergy <span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">specific reactions to Lunar Dust (LD) primarily looking at the key immune cell </span><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">responsiveness to LD itself, a simulant, and other common recall antigens or </span><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">polyclonal mitogens serving as positive controls. Human primary leukocytes </span><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">isolated from blood were used for all assessments. As the primary mediators of </span><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">allergy, eosinophils and basophils, are somewhat rare-percentages in the </span><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">peripheral blood of healthy individuals, two cell lines representing those </span><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">populations were also obtained and utilized.</span></div>
<div><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">Cellular responsiveness to co-culture in the presence of LD </span><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">was evaluated in the following different ways:</span></div><div><br></div><div><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">1.</span><font size="1" style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);"> </font><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">Assessing bulk levels of these cells before, and after culture via flow cytometry. </span></div><div><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">2. The induction of cell surface activation markers (flow cytometry).</span></div>
<div>3.<span style="font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 7pt; line-height: normal; font-family: "Times New Roman";">&nbsp;</span>Measuring soluble cytokines in culture supernatants (multiplex array)<o:p></o:p></div>
<div>4.<font face="Times New Roman" size="1"> </font>Measuring production of allergy-specific products IgE, leukotrienes, histamine (ELISA). <o:p></o:p></div>
<div>5.<span style="font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 7pt; line-height: normal; font-family: "Times New Roman";">&nbsp;</span>Monitoring proliferative/blastogenesis responses (CFSE method/Cytometry). <o:p></o:p></div>
<div>6.<span style="font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 7pt; line-height: normal; font-family: "Times New Roman";">&nbsp;</span>Monitoring cell-LD direct interactions (microscopy). <o:p></o:p></div>
<div><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">No changes were seen in cellular activation, cytokine production, </span><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">or cellular proliferation in the primary immune cells of healthy subjects in </span><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">response to LD. Allergy specific reactions indicated by increased histamine, </span><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">leukotrienes, or IgE were also not detected in subject whole blood cultures. </span><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">Proliferative responses of any whole blood populations to LD were not detected </span><span style="font-weight: var(--bs-body-font-weight); text-align: var(--bs-body-text-align);">in any subjects. </span></div>
<div>Direct activations of the eosinophils EOL-1 cell line was seen in response to LD; however activation was also detected in response to silica. The basophil cell line (KU812) responded to LD exposure by increased leukotriene production and the eosinophil cell line increased histamine after culture with LD. In short, assessments in primary human subject blood immune cells indicated no evidence whatsoever for sensitization, cellular responsiveness, nor ‘allergy’ to LD.<o:p></o:p></div><div><br></div><div>Assessments using purified ‘allergic’ cell lines, did yield some unique but mild responsiveness to LD. At least for monitoring activation for the Eosinophil cell line, responsiveness was observed to BOTH LD and silica. For leukotriene and histamine, responses were seen that seem to be LD specific. The Eo responsiveness to silica could indicate a manifestation of some sort of particulate response, however the Lt and histamine responses were more specific. Lunar dust exposure is inevitable for future lunar exploration missions and efforts to reduce the amount of LD exposure and countermeasures for addressing irritation will need to be put into place.<o:p></o:p></div></div>
        </div>

